requirements.description:
This feature checks the prepared Asset Data information before substantive review by the Asset Data Team.

It applies repeatable checks that can be defined and used consistently, such as whether required information has been provided, values use the expected format, values fall within an allowed range, related information is consistent and required relationships are present.

The checks will use the Asset Data expectation and information context prepared by the preceding FA5 features. Findings will identify the affected information and explain what has been found so that ADT can understand where attention or further action may be required.

The feature will distinguish:

a check that has passed;
a check that has identified a potential defect;
a check that cannot reach a reliable conclusion;
and a check that could not be completed because of a processing or technical problem.
The purpose is to identify repeatable problems earlier and focus ADT effort on information requiring professional interpretation and assurance. The findings will support ADT assessment and workload prioritisation, but they will not automatically determine whether a submission is accepted, corrected or rejected.

Material as-built variations shown through marked-up drawings remain subject to human interpretation by ADT. This feature does not assume that those variations can be identified reliably through automated assessment.

This feature enables an Asset Data Team member to apply accepted as-built changes and Asset Data through Electric Office and complete the operational Green Label journey.

Once the submitted information has been assessed and accepted, the ADT member will update the relevant design in EO to represent the agreed as-built position. They will enter or reuse the accepted structured Asset Data, review the resulting update and submit it through EO.

The EO-Maximo integration itself is owned by FA7. This feature enables the ADT member to see whether the submitted EO update has synchronised successfully with Maximo and to keep the Green Label case open where the result is pending, unsuccessful or cannot be confirmed.

Where FA7 identifies an integration, historic-data, hierarchy or other technical problem, the affected Green Label case will remain visible and linked to the technical action. ADT will be able to understand whether the matter:

is waiting for FA7 investigation or remediation;
has returned because the submitted business information needs further correction;
has completed successfully;
or has reached another explicitly agreed outcome.
The normal Green Label case will only be treated as operationally complete once the required Asset Data assessment and EO update are complete, successful Maximo synchronisation has been confirmed, and any outstanding business or technical exceptions have been resolved or explicitly dispositioned.

This feature does not execute or support the EO-Maximo integration. It uses the outcome supplied by FA7 to determine the continuing business state of the Green Label case.

This feature enables applicable PQS markers to be reconciled with the work and Asset Data activity to which they relate.

A PQS marker may indicate that a relevant change has taken place on the operational network, but it does not prove that the corresponding Asset Data has been received, processed or assured. ADT currently needs to establish whether the marker relates to known completed work, whether an Asset Data expectation or case exists, and whether the relevant update has reached its required outcome.

The feature will link a PQS marker to the corresponding originating work, Asset Data expectation and case where a reliable relationship can be established. It will show whether the Asset Data is:

expected but not yet received;
received and being prepared or assessed;
awaiting clarification, correction or technical action;
accepted and awaiting completion;
assured and complete;
or not yet matched to known work or an Asset Data case.
Where a marker cannot be matched, or the corresponding Asset Data remains outstanding, the item will become a controlled exception with a clear owner, age and next action. This will replace reliance on ad hoc investigation and repeated chasing through reports, email and Teams.

Where the corresponding Asset Data journey has completed successfully, the feature will provide a traceable assurance outcome to support the applicable BAU marker-clearance process.

This feature does not create or clear the PQS marker itself. Those responsibilities remain with the relevant operational and Control Room processes. It also does not repeat the normal FA5 and FA6 processing journey. It uses the work, case, workload and completion information already maintained by those capabilities.

This feature supports the controlled investigation and resolution of Lost, Found and Modified asset discrepancies.

An LFM discrepancy arises where an inspection or another operational activity identifies a difference between the physical asset position and the information held in the formal asset records. The discrepancy may relate to current or historic work, an outstanding Green Label case, an earlier work order, a duplicate exception, an unrecorded change or an underlying data or system problem.

The feature will first establish whether the discrepancy is already represented by known work, an existing Asset Data expectation, an active or completed Asset Data case, an earlier LFM record or another relevant exception. Where related activity exists, the discrepancy will be linked to it rather than automatically creating a separate investigation.

Where further investigation is required, the ADT member or other responsible investigator will be able to access the available work, Asset Data, design, asset-record and supporting evidence needed to understand the discrepancy. The investigation will determine whether the item:

can be resolved through an existing Asset Data case;
requires clarification or correction;
requires an asset or design update;
is a duplicate of existing activity;
requires FA7 stewardship, technical support or data remediation;
can be addressed through an agreed wider or bulk-remediation activity;
or cannot be resolved because sufficient supporting evidence is unavailable.
Each discrepancy will remain visible, owned and traceable until it reaches an explicit outcome. Where a supported factual resolution cannot be established, the item may only close as unresolved through an agreed and authorised business route, with the evidence considered and the reason for closure retained.

This feature does not own the inspection process that identifies the discrepancy or the initial BAU decision to create an LFM record. It also does not absorb general data cleansing, FA7 stewardship or technical remediation. It provides the Asset Data correlation, investigation and resolution journey required after an in-scope discrepancy has been raised.
